                "senderName": "NWS Houston/Galveston TX",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued October 2 at 8:36AM CDT until October 2 at 7:00PM CDT by NWS Houston/Galveston TX",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of south central and southeast Texas, including\nthe following areas, in south central Texas, Coastal Jackson and\nInland Jackson. In southeast Texas, Austin, Brazos, Burleson,\nCoastal Harris, Colorado, Fort Bend, Grimes, Inland Harris,\nMontgomery, Walker, Waller, Washington and Wharton.\n\n* WHEN...Until 7 PM CDT this evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Over the last 24 hours, much the watch area has seen between 3-6\ninches with a swath of 6-8+ inches observed from southwestern\nMontgomery County down through western Harris County. Showers and\nthunderstorms capable of producing heavy downpours will continue\nto impact the watch area through this evening. Embedded training\nrain rates of 2-4 inches per hour will be possible with the deep\ntropical airmass overhead. Locations that experience training\nstorms with heavier rainfall rates may  experience rapid runoff\nthat could result in flash flooding. Those living in areas prone\nto flooding should be prepared to take action should flooding\ndevelop. Stay weather aware and be ready to take action when\nFlash Flood Warnings are issued, particularly wireless emergency\nalert Flash Flood Warnings for considerable and/or catastrophic\nflooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.",
                "response": "Prepare",
